WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:07.010
VICTOR O'LAUGHLEN: Hi, everyone.

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:10.010
Victor O'Laughlen
and Sarthak Pattanaik

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:13.130
representing Bank of New
York Mellon's clearance

00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:15.560
and collateral
management business.

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:19.340
We're excited to present what
we've learned from our journey

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:23.630
in helping solve a key financial
services challenge that

00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:26.630
has the potential to
bring big-time benefits

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:30.980
to our clients and the US
treasury market as a whole,

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:34.250
all realized by leveraging
Google Cloud's powerful data

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:36.450
science platform.

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:38.790
As the global digital
business leader

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:41.070
for clearance and
collateral management,

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:44.730
leveraging data through
analytics and machine learning

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:47.430
is a key focus area
for our business

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:50.370
in order to enhance our
ability to improve the client

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:51.900
experience.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:54.690
Whether it's predicting
a failed transaction

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:57.510
or offering a prescriptive
analytics solution

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:00.780
to resolve it, our
future is firmly

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:05.250
planted in operating as
a data first company.

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:07.830
SARTHAK PATTANAIK: BNY
Mellon has a long history

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:10.140
of serving our clients.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:13.650
As a CIO of clearance
and collateral,

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:18.860
my responsibility is to focus
on growing this business.

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:23.490
But it is important for
us to know how we got here

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:27.260
and how the business grew
to where it is today.

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:31.310
It will share the importance
of this exciting new service

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:32.451
that we are building.

00:01:35.478 --> 00:01:37.020
VICTOR O'LAUGHLEN:
It actually starts

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:41.310
with the average investor
who, for example, saves

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:43.830
into a 401(k).

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:47.580
If the investor rebalances
her 401(k) account,

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:50.520
shifting from equities
in favor of bonds,

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:55.080
then her 401(k) administrator
may get those bonds

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:57.690
by contacting their
trading desk, who

00:01:57.690 --> 00:02:01.500
in turn goes to the
bond market and says,

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:08.520
I'd like to buy treasury bond
ABC for 95% of the face value.

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:13.260
Buyer and seller, named
EB, make their offers,

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:15.450
and a trade is arranged.

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:20.960
The buyer and seller agree
to swap the bond for cash.

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:24.190
And they notify a third
party service provider

00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:27.010
of the agreement
because they want

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:31.090
that service provider to manage
the actual exchange of bond

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:33.370
for cash at the end of the day.

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:39.120
The fund admin will
agree to many such trades

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:40.840
throughout the day.

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:45.330
In most cases that exchange
is successfully completed,

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:48.380
but sometimes it's not.

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:50.750
A counterparty may
not be able to deliver

00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:53.240
the bond due to some
unforeseen event

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:56.160
occurring after the agreement.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:59.280
But the investor still
needs their bond.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:01.800
The fund admin keeps
some extra on hand

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:05.670
to make the investor whole
in case of a settlement fail.

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:09.540
But how many extra
should the admin keep?

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:14.580
Well, that depends on how often
a settlement failure occurs.

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:17.110
If the admin knew
that, they could right

00:03:17.110 --> 00:03:20.020
size their stockpile and
free up valuable cash.

00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:27.370
This cash could improve market
liquidity by buying and selling

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:31.810
more bonds or fund the
development of new services

00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:33.880
for the investor.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:37.780
So predicting
settlement fails is key,

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:41.640
but that would require a
total view of the market.

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:43.440
The fund admin
doesn't have that,

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:47.490
but the clearing and
settlement service does.

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:51.690
BNY Mellon is that clearing
and settlement service.

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:54.070
Because we're in the
center of the market,

00:03:54.070 --> 00:03:56.560
we have a total market view.

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:01.380
And with that view plus Google
Cloud's powerful analytics,

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:03.240
we can predict which
trades will fail

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:06.210
to settle, allowing
the admin to predict

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:09.760
how many spare treasuries
to keep on hand.

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:13.420
Of course, BNY Mellon didn't
begin in the cloud era.

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:16.820
We had a 236 year head start.

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:22.270
In 1784, we were founded by
then US treasurer Alexander

00:04:22.270 --> 00:04:23.710
Hamilton.

00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:26.880
Some of you may have
seen the musical.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:31.440
In 1789, the first loan
brought to the US government

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:37.180
was provided by BNYM in the
form of a treasury security.

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:39.880
Fast forward to
today, and treasuries

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:43.870
are the largest and most liquid
fixed market in the world,

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:47.650
with BNY Mellon still deeply
involved in this market,

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:50.530
clearing and settling
over $9 trillion in Fed

00:04:50.530 --> 00:04:51.940
eligible securities a day.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:59.210
Because of our important role
in the financial markets,

00:04:59.210 --> 00:05:01.550
a core mission of
our business is

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:04.280
to invest in
resiliency and value

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:09.680
added services that help create
efficiency and stability.

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:12.500
And helping solve the
Fed eligible securities

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:17.220
fails challenge is a
prime example of that.

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:19.430
So we kicked off of
an investigation,

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:22.580
or what we call "formed
a hypothesis," into how

00:05:22.580 --> 00:05:26.350
we might help predict
these settlement fails.

00:05:26.350 --> 00:05:28.570
The fails vary from day to day.

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:31.110
But generally, they
amount to 1% to 2%

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:36.300
of approximately $4.5 trillion
in security deliveries.

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:39.790
This percentage seems like
a small number, and it is.

00:05:39.790 --> 00:05:42.820
But due to the sheer
size of this market,

00:05:42.820 --> 00:05:45.120
it amounts to
approximately $70 billion

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:47.940
of settlement fails a day.

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:50.250
Transactions that
do not successfully

00:05:50.250 --> 00:05:53.460
settle by 3:00 PM
Eastern Standard Time

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:56.200
each day are considered fails.

00:05:56.200 --> 00:06:00.490
So we aim to predict the
fails at 1:15 PM each day,

00:06:00.490 --> 00:06:03.690
providing approximately
two hours notice.

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:08.630
Sarthak, can you tell us
how we went about this?

00:06:08.630 --> 00:06:10.990
SARTHAK PATTANAIK: There are
four stages to our machine

00:06:10.990 --> 00:06:13.040
learning process.

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:16.630
Stage one is focused on
data ingestion, where

00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:20.140
we build a data
pipeline to move data

00:06:20.140 --> 00:06:22.780
from our on-prem
transactional system,

00:06:22.780 --> 00:06:26.140
to our on-prem data [INAUDIBLE]
all the way to Google Cloud

00:06:26.140 --> 00:06:28.080
storage.

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:32.970
In the second stage, we are
focused on data preparation.

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:37.340
The first part of it is ensuring
good data quality controls.

00:06:37.340 --> 00:06:41.100
And the second part of it
is feature engineering.

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:43.470
In the feature
engineering step, we

00:06:43.470 --> 00:06:46.890
start off with
transactional information,

00:06:46.890 --> 00:06:50.910
things like account,
security information,

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:53.320
and transactional information.

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:58.250
And then we iterate across
a multifunctional group,

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:03.820
try to get more information from
the clients, from the business,

00:07:03.820 --> 00:07:07.460
from products, from developers,
as well as from the data

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:09.430
engineers.

00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:12.220
At the end of
multiple iterations,

00:07:12.220 --> 00:07:15.900
we lined it up
with 51 variables.

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:18.930
44 of them, we have
something that we identified

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:21.000
as part of those iterations.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:25.600
And most of these variables
were economic in nature, things

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:31.030
like demand supply variables,
liquidity variables,

00:07:31.030 --> 00:07:34.280
and transaction
velocity variables.

00:07:34.280 --> 00:07:38.530
The third stage is
around model building.

00:07:38.530 --> 00:07:40.570
And when we are
building the model,

00:07:40.570 --> 00:07:43.930
we were targeting three
objective functions--

00:07:43.930 --> 00:07:49.690
one, accuracy, second,
performance, and third,

00:07:49.690 --> 00:07:51.620
interpretability.

00:07:51.620 --> 00:07:53.630
And we evaluated
a whole plethora

00:07:53.630 --> 00:07:56.990
of models from
simple regression,

00:07:56.990 --> 00:08:01.870
to three base models, all
the way to neural net.

00:08:01.870 --> 00:08:04.180
As we [INAUDIBLE]
over the models,

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:08.780
we did finally settle
with the light GBM model.

00:08:08.780 --> 00:08:12.460
And the reason for that is
the balance between accuracy

00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:14.960
and interpretability.

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:18.830
Our clients were very
interested to use a model which

00:08:18.830 --> 00:08:22.070
provides them
directionality as to why

00:08:22.070 --> 00:08:25.430
a transaction has a
higher chance of failure

00:08:25.430 --> 00:08:28.340
than another transaction.

00:08:28.340 --> 00:08:32.150
The fourth stage is building
the production pipeline.

00:08:32.150 --> 00:08:35.960
Once we built a model
which would train and test,

00:08:35.960 --> 00:08:40.640
having an automation to take the
output of the model, the output

00:08:40.640 --> 00:08:44.510
coefficients into a
production system,

00:08:44.510 --> 00:08:46.780
and ensuring that
that is seamless,

00:08:46.780 --> 00:08:49.340
was critical for our success.

00:08:49.340 --> 00:08:53.540
Also important was the frequency
as we retrained the model

00:08:53.540 --> 00:08:57.560
every three months, and
also going through the model

00:08:57.560 --> 00:09:00.470
validation to ensure
that the model did

00:09:00.470 --> 00:09:03.150
what it's supposed to do.

00:09:03.150 --> 00:09:06.510
What did we learn from
this entire process?

00:09:06.510 --> 00:09:11.230
Three things-- first
is data culture.

00:09:11.230 --> 00:09:13.810
We are trying to
transform ourselves

00:09:13.810 --> 00:09:16.270
from a transaction-based
organization

00:09:16.270 --> 00:09:18.960
to a data-driven organization.

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:20.840
And if you think
about data, we think

00:09:20.840 --> 00:09:26.810
about the five Vs, which is
volume, velocity, variety,

00:09:26.810 --> 00:09:29.910
veracity, and value.

00:09:29.910 --> 00:09:32.940
Veracity was the most
important concept for us

00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:38.160
because we wanted to build
is solid data control process

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:41.660
and solid quality
controls upfront.

00:09:41.660 --> 00:09:44.750
The second thing we learned
was around our transactional

00:09:44.750 --> 00:09:47.720
systems and storing everything.

00:09:47.720 --> 00:09:50.930
Traditional transaction
systems, we focused a lot

00:09:50.930 --> 00:09:53.000
on storing our inputs.

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:55.170
And then we have our algorithms.

00:09:55.170 --> 00:09:57.440
And we stored the
outputs, which is

00:09:57.440 --> 00:09:59.720
used for client
reporting, our end

00:09:59.720 --> 00:10:02.070
of the regulatory reporting.

00:10:02.070 --> 00:10:04.190
But as a part of
this process, we

00:10:04.190 --> 00:10:08.480
tried to store information
at every point in time

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:10.820
during the algorithm
for every decision

00:10:10.820 --> 00:10:13.250
point the algorithm took.

00:10:13.250 --> 00:10:15.740
As a result, we captured
inordinate amount

00:10:15.740 --> 00:10:21.440
of risk, credit usage,
inventory, position,

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:26.570
and cash information because we
do not know which of this data

00:10:26.570 --> 00:10:30.500
is going to be useful
for our next experiment.

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:33.530
Thirdly, we realized
very quickly

00:10:33.530 --> 00:10:36.020
that the success
of this project has

00:10:36.020 --> 00:10:40.280
to be a mix of expertise
from the data engineers,

00:10:40.280 --> 00:10:44.170
as well as the subject
matter experts.

00:10:44.170 --> 00:10:46.660
As we were iterating,
there are a couple

00:10:46.660 --> 00:10:51.880
of sprints where we
had 95% plus accuracy.

00:10:51.880 --> 00:10:54.750
And when we went back
and looked at it,

00:10:54.750 --> 00:10:59.400
we actually found that we
are predicting the past.

00:10:59.400 --> 00:11:01.060
So that was an
interesting thing,

00:11:01.060 --> 00:11:03.890
that when your model
is significantly good,

00:11:03.890 --> 00:11:07.060
go back and check your model.

00:11:07.060 --> 00:11:10.560
The second thing in this
entire process that we learned

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:13.220
was the concept of agility.

00:11:13.220 --> 00:11:15.950
This is a multifunctional team.

00:11:15.950 --> 00:11:21.620
And the goal of the team was
to have small experiments

00:11:21.620 --> 00:11:26.010
in a piecemeal fashion and have
a hypothesis for each of them.

00:11:26.010 --> 00:11:29.180
And as we addressed, as
we hit those hypotheses,

00:11:29.180 --> 00:11:34.790
we moved forward, or we go back
and restart with our new set

00:11:34.790 --> 00:11:38.580
of sets of hypotheses.

00:11:38.580 --> 00:11:41.040
So for that to happen,
it is important

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:43.100
that infrastructure
is not [INAUDIBLE],,

00:11:43.100 --> 00:11:46.630
and infrastructure is
an enabler for that.

00:11:46.630 --> 00:11:48.360
And the third thing
that we learned

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:51.070
is digital transformation.

00:11:51.070 --> 00:11:53.290
This is not about technology.

00:11:53.290 --> 00:11:58.410
It's about people, process,
and technology together.

00:11:58.410 --> 00:12:00.970
When we build machine
learning projects,

00:12:00.970 --> 00:12:02.640
those are not deterministic.

00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:04.350
These are exploratory.

00:12:04.350 --> 00:12:08.280
These do not have a
requirements document, a design,

00:12:08.280 --> 00:12:12.690
a development, and a testing
process like a waterfall.

00:12:12.690 --> 00:12:16.140
You have to think about
small experiments.

00:12:16.140 --> 00:12:19.200
You have to look at hypotheses.

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:20.880
And you have to iterate.

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:25.420
So fast iteration is
key to our success.

00:12:25.420 --> 00:12:28.640
Now let's talk about results.

00:12:28.640 --> 00:12:36.530
Our target was to predict 40%
of the fails with 80% precision.

00:12:36.530 --> 00:12:42.560
And happy to say that we are
able to hit those objectives.

00:12:42.560 --> 00:12:45.410
Now, I'll hand it over to
Victor to talk about what's

00:12:45.410 --> 00:12:46.850
next in the service.

00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:51.440
VICTOR O'LAUGHLEN:
Thanks, Sarthak.

00:12:51.440 --> 00:12:54.260
So next is a client trial.

00:12:54.260 --> 00:12:58.780
After approximately a year
of development and testing,

00:12:58.780 --> 00:13:00.470
we are now currently
in the process

00:13:00.470 --> 00:13:04.310
of trialing this service
with our key clients.

00:13:04.310 --> 00:13:07.220
This includes interacting
with the service

00:13:07.220 --> 00:13:10.820
in a production-like
environment and evaluating

00:13:10.820 --> 00:13:13.040
the benefits of the
service so that they

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:17.820
can trust that the service
is accurate and consistent.

00:13:17.820 --> 00:13:20.440
As the client provides
feedback to us,

00:13:20.440 --> 00:13:24.840
we can seek out opportunities
to make client-specific model

00:13:24.840 --> 00:13:26.520
optimizations.

00:13:26.520 --> 00:13:28.800
Are there specific
operational nuances

00:13:28.800 --> 00:13:30.930
about that particular
client that

00:13:30.930 --> 00:13:33.450
are required in order for
us, and from a modeling

00:13:33.450 --> 00:13:37.950
perspective, in order for us to
refine the precision or recall?

00:13:37.950 --> 00:13:40.470
In addition, we will
continue iterating

00:13:40.470 --> 00:13:44.680
on improving the service
through various market cycles.

00:13:44.680 --> 00:13:46.350
For example, in our
line of business,

00:13:46.350 --> 00:13:48.510
month ends, quarter
ends, year ends

00:13:48.510 --> 00:13:50.700
are important times
for our clients.

00:13:50.700 --> 00:13:52.920
And the dynamics
of trading change,

00:13:52.920 --> 00:13:54.960
therefore making it
more difficult for us

00:13:54.960 --> 00:13:57.180
to predict settlement fails.

00:13:57.180 --> 00:14:00.180
But by identifying
these different cycles,

00:14:00.180 --> 00:14:02.850
adjusting for the
different training periods,

00:14:02.850 --> 00:14:04.680
and looking at different
model variables,

00:14:04.680 --> 00:14:07.260
we can find opportunities
to improve the model

00:14:07.260 --> 00:14:09.840
during those time periods.

00:14:09.840 --> 00:14:13.570
And finally, as we
predict outcomes,

00:14:13.570 --> 00:14:16.060
we can then identify
opportunities

00:14:16.060 --> 00:14:19.270
to have prescriptive
analytics integrated

00:14:19.270 --> 00:14:22.870
into those predictions to
help mitigate the fails, which

00:14:22.870 --> 00:14:26.020
will then allow for our
clients to get more value out

00:14:26.020 --> 00:14:28.470
of this service.

00:14:28.470 --> 00:14:31.300
Now finally, we'll
hand it over to Sarthak

00:14:31.300 --> 00:14:35.120
to talk about, why Google Cloud?

00:14:35.120 --> 00:14:36.950
SARTHAK PATTANAIK:
Why Google Cloud?

00:14:36.950 --> 00:14:40.040
So first, I want to
talk about, why cloud?

00:14:40.040 --> 00:14:42.560
If you think about
this project, the two

00:14:42.560 --> 00:14:48.600
focus areas where around
scalability and agility,

00:14:48.600 --> 00:14:53.180
the ability for us to run
complex machine learning models

00:14:53.180 --> 00:14:57.240
requiring high
infrastructure requirements.

00:14:57.240 --> 00:15:01.160
And if we had to
do it on-prem, it

00:15:01.160 --> 00:15:05.630
would have taken us significant
time, as well as it would not

00:15:05.630 --> 00:15:08.670
have been cost efficient.

00:15:08.670 --> 00:15:13.140
So cloud was a natural
solution for this project.

00:15:13.140 --> 00:15:15.160
Why Google Cloud?

00:15:15.160 --> 00:15:17.760
There are a couple
of reasons here.

00:15:17.760 --> 00:15:21.100
One is Google BigQuery.

00:15:21.100 --> 00:15:25.860
BigQuery is highly scalable
and extremely performant.

00:15:25.860 --> 00:15:28.050
Our developers and
business analysts

00:15:28.050 --> 00:15:33.330
used to run queries on our
on-prem databases of two

00:15:33.330 --> 00:15:36.240
to three days of
transaction data.

00:15:36.240 --> 00:15:38.670
On Google Cloud
BigQuery, they were

00:15:38.670 --> 00:15:41.970
able to run the same
set of queries over two

00:15:41.970 --> 00:15:45.180
to three years at a
transaction level.

00:15:45.180 --> 00:15:48.410
And the performance was
very similar to two to three

00:15:48.410 --> 00:15:51.830
days on-prem.

00:15:51.830 --> 00:15:56.150
The second thing was
about collaboration.

00:15:56.150 --> 00:15:59.840
This was a perfect opportunity
for the Google engineers

00:15:59.840 --> 00:16:04.190
and the BNY Mellon
engineers to work together.

00:16:04.190 --> 00:16:07.420
Both of us focused on data.

00:16:07.420 --> 00:16:10.000
How do we build
strong data practices

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:15.700
in terms of data ingestion,
data storage, data indexing,

00:16:15.700 --> 00:16:20.060
as well as delivering
data to our clients?

00:16:20.060 --> 00:16:24.340
It was fascinating to watch
both the teams come together

00:16:24.340 --> 00:16:27.890
and solve a business problem.

00:16:27.890 --> 00:16:29.980
Thank you for watching
this, and hope this

00:16:29.980 --> 00:16:33.200
was helpful for yourselves.

